From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 07:31:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 07/17] luks2: fixes from a correctness review: transactions,
 durability, in-use checks

- Header writes are ordered and flushed: luks2_device_io has a flush hook
  (luks2_fd_flush(): fsync() for files, B_FLUSH_DRIVE_CACHE for Haiku
  devices, whose fsync() does nothing). Keyslot material is flushed before
  the header that points to it, each header copy before the other is
  touched (a damaged or older copy first), a removed keyslot's area is
  wiped after the header no longer points to it; errors reach the caller.
- The library reads both copies again before writing and refuses with
  LUKS2_ERROR_CHANGED when they changed; the luks command and
  EncryptedVolume take an exclusive flock() on the device for a change, so
  a second command is refused instead of racing (two removekeys could both
  pass the last-key check).
- The reader tries the other header copy only when the newer one's
  metadata is damaged, not when it asks for an unsupported feature (the
  older copy may describe a layout that no longer holds, and the next
  change wrote it over both); dump names the copy it read.
- removekey counts only keyslots bound to the data segment; dump marks
  unbound ones.
- luks2_partition_in_use() (mounted, has partitions, open as a LUKS2
  volume): EncryptedVolume checks it when the window opens and again in
  the worker before formatting; luks format and luks write check it too
  (O_EXCL for Linux block devices).
- luks write refuses a file that doesn't fit or isn't whole sectors before
  writing anything; its final flush is checked.
- EncryptedVolume refuses passphrases over 512 bytes instead of cutting
  them; its error alert no longer blocks the application.
- Boot: the loader finds a volume by its secondary header too
  (luks2_probe()), and the kernel takes the UUID from the driver's boot
  module (volume_uuid()), which reads the header like the driver, instead
  of the primary copy's raw bytes. Tracker and DriveSetup see a volume
  whose primary magic is damaged.

Tests: crash_test (power lost after every write and flush, every fate of
the unflushed writes), 21 new cryptsetup checks, new cases in the QEMU
driver, boot (damaged primary UUID and magic) and GUI tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
